(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-pulmonary-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"Pulmonary Physiology — Quiz 2 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Throughout the normal breathing cycle, intrapleural pressure remains...","choices":["higher than alveolar pressure at all times under most conditions throughout the body","exactly equal to atmospheric pressure during inspiration","positive during quiet expiration to force air out","more negative than alveolar pressure, keeping the lung against the chest wall"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intrapleural pressure stays below (more negative than) alveolar pressure across the cycle; this transpulmonary pressure difference holds the lung expanded against the chest wall and prevents it from collapsing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 16-19"},{"q":"The diaphragm receives its motor innervation from the...","choices":["phrenic nerve, arising from cervical spinal segments","sympathetic chain, acting through spinal ganglia","vagus nerve, which also controls the heart rate","intercostal nerves that supply only the rib muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diaphragm is driven by the phrenic nerve, which originates from cervical spinal segments (about C3–C5); this is why high cervical spinal cord injury can paralyze the diaphragm and abolish spontaneous breathing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-13"},{"q":"Airway radius has a powerful effect on airflow because resistance is...","choices":["independent of radius and set only by the air's viscosity","directly proportional to the radius of the airway","inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius","proportional to the square of the airway's length"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: by Poiseuille's relationship, resistance varies with 1/r^4, so small radius changes greatly affect flow. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 16"},{"q":"Emphysema and pulmonary fibrosis affect compliance in opposite ways: emphysema tends to...","choices":["increase compliance by destroying elastic tissue, while fibrosis stiffens the lung","have no measurable effect on lung compliance at all as it is usually described here","stiffen the lung, while fibrosis makes it easier to inflate","make the lung easier to inflate and to recoil normally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and elastic fibers, so the lung becomes floppy and easier to inflate (higher compliance) but recoils poorly; fibrosis lays down stiff tissue, lowering compliance so the lung is harder to inflate. They alter lung mechanics in opposite directions. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 19-21"},{"q":"Intrapleural pressure is normally negative (sub-atmospheric), which...","choices":["has no effect on whether the lungs stay inflated","keeps the lungs expanded against their tendency to recoil inward","forces the lungs to collapse against the chest wall","actively pushes the air outward during quiet resting inspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The negative intrapleural pressure results from the lung's inward recoil opposing the chest wall's outward pull; this sub-atmospheric pressure holds the lungs expanded and coupled to the chest wall throughout the breathing cycle. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 14, 17-19"},{"q":"Insufficient surfactant, as in a premature newborn, leads to breathing difficulty because...","choices":["gas exchange speeds up beyond what the blood can handle","surface tension in the alveoli drops too low under most conditions throughout the body","alveoli tend to collapse, so much greater effort is needed to reinflate them","the alveoli become too easy to inflate and overexpand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Without adequate surfactant, alveolar surface tension is high; alveoli collapse at the end of expiration, and reopening them requires large pressures, greatly increasing the work of breathing — the basis of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-24"},{"q":"Surfactant stabilizes small alveoli by...","choices":["increasing the radius of every alveolus throughout the lung","lowering surface tension so small alveoli resist collapsing into larger ones","raising surface tension uniformly across the alveoli of every possible size","thickening the alveolar fluid lining so as to slow the diffusion of gases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: surfactant reduces surface tension more in small alveoli, preventing them from emptying into larger ones (Laplace). — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"By lowering surface tension, surfactant...","choices":["raises the surface tension evenly across all of the alveoli regardless of size","reduces the effort needed to inflate the lungs and prevents alveolar collapse","thickens the fluid layer to slow gas exchange","increases the work of breathing to strengthen the lungs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surfactant lowers alveolar surface tension, which raises compliance (making the lungs easier to inflate) and stabilizes alveoli of different sizes so small ones do not empty into larger ones; without it, the work of breathing rises and alveoli collapse (as in neonatal respiratory distress). — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Inspiratory reserve volume is the air that...","choices":["remains in the lungs after a maximal expiration","is exhaled during a single quiet resting breath","can still be exhaled beyond a normal quiet resting expiration","can still be inhaled beyond a normal quiet inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inspiratory reserve volume is the additional air that can be forcibly inhaled after a normal tidal inspiration; it is the inspiratory 'reserve' available above the resting breath. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 27-29"},{"q":"Vital capacity is best described as...","choices":["the air still remaining in the lungs after a maximal forced expiration","the maximum air expired after a maximal inspiration (IRV + VT + ERV)","the air remaining after a normal quiet expiration","the volume of air that is moved during a single normal quiet resting breath"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: vital capacity is the greatest volume that can be expelled after a maximal inspiration, the sum of IRV, tidal volume, and ERV. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 29"},{"q":"Anatomic dead space reduces the efficiency of ventilation because...","choices":["it holds air that has already fully equilibrated with pulmonary blood","air in the conducting airways never reaches alveoli for gas exchange","it is the region where most oxygen uptake actually takes place","it represents alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused by blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the ~150 mL in conducting airways does not participate in gas exchange, so alveolar ventilation is less than minute ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"In a healthy lung at rest, blood in the pulmonary capillary equilibrates with alveolar oxygen...","choices":["well before it leaves the capillary, giving a large safety margin","only at the very last instant before leaving the capillary","never fully, so arterial blood is always desaturated","only during vigorous exercise, and essentially never while at rest"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At rest, oxygen equilibrates between alveolus and capillary blood in about a third of the transit time, so equilibration is complete long before the blood exits the capillary; this reserve is the safety factor that is eroded in disease or reduced with very fast transit during exercise. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 67-69"},{"q":"Ventilation-perfusion matching improves gas exchange by...","choices":["sending essentially all blood flow to the very apex of the upright lung","ensuring blood always flows to alveoli that receive no air during this particular process","keeping ventilation constant regardless of local blood flow","aligning airflow and blood flow so alveoli are neither wasted nor underused"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Efficient exchange requires that alveolar ventilation and capillary perfusion be matched; local mechanisms (such as hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction) direct blood toward well-ventilated alveoli, minimizing both wasted ventilation and wasted perfusion. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 43, 64-65"},{"q":"Fluid accumulating in the interstitial space of the respiratory membrane would...","choices":["increase the diffusion distance and slow gas exchange","have no effect on the rate of gas diffusion","convert the membrane into an active transport surface","shorten the diffusion distance and speed exchange"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. By thickening the barrier that gases must cross, interstitial or alveolar fluid (as in pulmonary edema) lengthens the diffusion distance; per Fick's law, this reduces the rate of gas exchange and can impair oxygenation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 60-62"},{"q":"Gas exchange across the respiratory membrane is efficient largely because the total alveolar surface is...","choices":["very small but compensated by thickness","large but separated from blood by cartilage","thin but limited to a few large air sacs","very large while the membrane is extremely thin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lungs contain hundreds of millions of alveoli providing an enormous total surface area, and the respiratory membrane is only about 0.2 micrometers thick; large area and short diffusion distance together maximize the rate of gas exchange. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 57-60"},{"q":"Central chemoreceptors in the brainstem adjust ventilation mainly in response to...","choices":["changes in CO2 and H+ within the surrounding brain fluid","stretch signals arriving from receptors located in the lung","changes in arterial oxygen sensed directly within the medulla","blood pressure changes detected at the carotid sinus baroreceptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: central chemoreceptors respond primarily to CO2 and H+ in brain extracellular fluid, the strongest minute-to-minute drive. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91, 96"},{"q":"The pontine (pneumotaxic) center influences breathing mainly by...","choices":["contracting the diaphragm directly through the phrenic nerve","generating the fundamental breathing rhythm on its own","sensing arterial oxygen at the carotid bodies under most conditions in the body","limiting the duration of inspiration and helping set the breathing rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pneumotaxic center in the pons sends signals that switch off inspiration, thereby limiting inspiratory time; a stronger signal shortens inspiration and quickens the rate, so this center helps fine-tune the rhythm generated by the medulla. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 88-90"},{"q":"Peripheral chemoreceptors also respond to blood pH, so a fall in pH (acidosis) tends to...","choices":["have no effect on breathing, since only CO2 matters during this process","stimulate ventilation, helping to remove CO2 and correct the pH","cause the kidneys to immediately exhale the excess acid","suppress ventilation, allowing CO2 to accumulate further"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The carotid and aortic bodies sense a fall in pH and increase their signaling to the respiratory center; the resulting rise in ventilation blows off CO2, which lowers carbonic acid and helps raise the pH back toward normal — a respiratory compensation for acidosis. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 95-99"},{"q":"Nearly all of the oxygen in blood is carried...","choices":["as bicarbonate formed from the reaction of oxygen and water","dissolved in the plasma, with hemoglobin contributing very little","chemically combined with plasma proteins other than hemoglobin","bound to hemoglobin, with only a small fraction dissolved in plasma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the great majority of oxygen is carried on hemoglobin; only a small amount is physically dissolved. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 76-77"},{"q":"Carbon monoxide impairs oxygen delivery because it...","choices":["binds hemoglobin far more tightly than oxygen, blocking the O2 sites","destroys the heme iron so that hemoglobin can never re-form","dissolves oxygen out of the plasma into the expired air","stimulates excessive oxygen release before blood reaches the tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin with much greater affinity than oxygen, occupying binding sites and reducing O2 carriage. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 82"},{"q":"A leftward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve, indicating higher affinity, occurs with...","choices":["heavy exercise that raises the tissue temperature","decreased temperature, decreased CO2, and the presence of fetal hemoglobin","a rise in CO2 and hydrogen ion concentration as it is usually described here","increased temperature and increased acidity in the tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lower temperature, lower CO2/acidity, decreased 2,3-BPG, fetal hemoglobin, and carbon monoxide all shift the curve leftward, raising oxygen affinity so hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly and releases it less readily at the tissues. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 80-82"},{"q":"Which correctly describes CO2 movement?","choices":["CO2 is carried in the blood without ever actually leaving it at all","CO2 diffuses from blood into tissues at both sites","CO2 diffuses from tissues into blood, then from blood into alveoli","CO2 moves from alveoli into blood in the lungs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metabolizing tissues have a high PCO2, so CO2 diffuses into the blood; in the lungs, alveolar PCO2 is lower than that of venous blood, so CO2 diffuses from blood into the alveoli to be exhaled. Each step follows the partial-pressure gradient. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 73-74, 86-87"},{"q":"Comparing alveolar gas with the systemic venous blood arriving at the lungs, the alveoli have...","choices":["a lower oxygen and a higher carbon dioxide partial pressure than that blood","no oxygen at all, so none can diffuse into the blood","a higher oxygen and a lower carbon dioxide partial pressure than that blood","identical oxygen and carbon dioxide pressures to that blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alveolar air has higher PO2 and lower PCO2 than the venous blood entering the pulmonary capillaries; these opposing gradients drive oxygen into the blood and carbon dioxide out of it, so blood leaves the lungs oxygenated and CO2-depleted. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 63-66, 71-74"},{"q":"Oxygen moves from capillary blood into a metabolizing cell because...","choices":["the cell's PO2 is lower than that of the arriving blood","blood PO2 is always lower than the cell's PO2","the cell actively pumps oxygen across its membrane","carbon dioxide actively pushes the oxygen into the cell"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Actively metabolizing cells consume oxygen, keeping intracellular PO2 low; oxygen therefore diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient from the higher-PO2 capillary blood into the cell, with no active transport required. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 71-73"},{"q":"The Haldane effect describes how...","choices":["carbon monoxide competitively displaces carbon dioxide out of the plasma","bicarbonate physically prevents hemoglobin from carrying oxygen","oxygenating hemoglobin in the lungs promotes the release of carbon dioxide","high carbon dioxide levels force hemoglobin to bind still more oxygen molecules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the Haldane effect is that binding oxygen reduces hemoglobin's affinity for CO2, aiding CO2 release in the lungs. — Pulmonary I & II, slide 85"},{"q":"As bicarbonate leaves the red cell in exchange for chloride (the chloride shift), the purpose is to...","choices":["prevent bicarbonate from ever entering the plasma","bind oxygen more tightly to the hemoglobin inside the red cell","generate ATP for the red cell's own energy needs","maintain electrical balance as CO2 is converted for transport"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase generates bicarbonate inside the red cell; as HCO3- diffuses out into plasma, chloride moves in to preserve electrical neutrality (the chloride shift), allowing large amounts of CO2 to be carried as plasma bicarbonate. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 86-87"},{"q":"The chloride shift accompanies bicarbonate handling because, as bicarbonate leaves the red cell,...","choices":["sodium leaves the cell to replace the lost charge","chloride enters the cell to keep it electrically balanced","the cell manufactures new hemoglobin to compensate","oxygen is forced out of the cell in exchange in most cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase generates bicarbonate inside the red cell; as bicarbonate diffuses out into plasma, chloride moves in (the chloride, or Hamburger, shift) to preserve electrical neutrality, allowing large amounts of CO2 to be carried as plasma bicarbonate. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-87"},{"q":"The tight link between CO2 and pH means that increasing ventilation...","choices":["adds CO2 and thereby raises hydrogen ion concentration","has no relationship to blood acid-base status","removes CO2 and thereby reduces hydrogen ion concentration","boosts oxygen saturation slightly without altering blood pH at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid (and thus H+), increasing ventilation removes CO2, lowers carbonic acid, and reduces hydrogen ion concentration, raising pH; this is the respiratory system's rapid lever on acid-base balance. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92"},{"q":"Voluntary hyperventilation, by removing extra CO2, tends to...","choices":["raise blood pH, producing a respiratory alkalosis","leave blood pH completely unchanged","raise CO2 levels in the blood in the body under most conditions","lower blood pH, producing a respiratory acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hyperventilation blows off CO2, lowering carbonic acid and hydrogen ion concentration, so blood pH rises — a respiratory alkalosis. This illustrates how ventilation directly influences acid-base balance through CO2. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"Central chemoreceptors respond to blood CO2 indirectly because CO2...","choices":["is sensed only after it has been converted into oxygen in the brain","cannot cross into the brain and must be sensed peripherally","crosses into brain fluid and forms H+, which the receptors detect","binds directly to the chemoreceptor without any change"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. CO2 readily crosses the blood-brain barrier into the brain extracellular fluid, where carbonic anhydrase converts it to carbonic acid and then H+; the central chemoreceptors actually sense that H+, giving CO2 its powerful (if indirect) effect on ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91, 96-98"}]};
